-- pregnancy week 11: Iron, absorbed well
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('pregnancy',
   11,
   'first',
   NULL,
   'Iron, absorbed well',
   '"Pair plant iron with vitamin C, give tea and coffee some space, and take iron or IFA exactly as advised when it''s prescribed."',
   'Iron helps carry oxygen to you and your growing baby. Pregnancy substantially increases iron requirements. The dietary target for pregnancy is 27mg a day (ICMR-NIN, 2020), while India''s national programme provides a daily IFA tablet containing 60mg elemental iron and 500mcg folic acid from the second trimester. A supplement dose shouldn''t be compared directly with a food target — absorption varies, and the programme is designed to prevent anaemia in a population where low iron stores and anaemia are common, not because pregnancy itself demands a different amount in India specifically. Heme iron (from meat, poultry, and fish) is absorbed far more efficiently than non-heme iron (from plant foods like dals, leafy greens, and jaggery) — but both matter, and pairing plant-based iron with vitamin C can make a real difference. If you eat meat, fish, or eggs: Meat, poultry, and fish are genuinely valuable this week — they''re the only sources of heme iron, which your body absorbs far more efficiently than the non-heme iron in plant foods. Eggs contain iron too, but in the non-heme form, so they add some value without matching meat or fish. The one exception, as with folate last week, is liver — very rich in iron but also concentrated in vitamin A, so it''s better skipped in pregnancy.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Today: A couple of iron-containing foods across the day is a reasonable place to start. India''s routine IFA programme generally begins in the second trimester, although your care team may prescribe iron earlier if you need it. Don''t start, stop, or change an iron supplement on your own.", "breakfast_a": "A moong or besan chilla with tomato chutney, or an iron-fortified cereal with fruit.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a fully cooked egg alongside.", "lunch_a": "Roti, rajma or chana dal, and a cooked leafy vegetable (palak or methi).", "lunch_b": "The same roti, dal, and vegetable, with a portion of chicken or mutton curry (cooked through) added alongside — a genuine heme-iron addition.", "nourishment_break_a": "A handful of dates and a small orange.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted sesame or pumpkin seeds.", "dinner_a": "Palak dal, or a rajma-and-greens combination, with roti.", "dinner_b": "A fish or mutton curry (cooked through) with roti, plus a side of dal or greens to keep today''s iron source in place.", "still_hungry": "Fruit, toast, or another familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Pair it, don''t block it", "notes": "Today: Vitamin C genuinely helps your body absorb non-heme (plant-based) iron from food — even a modest amount, like a squeeze of lemon or a side of citrus, can meaningfully increase how much you take up, whether or not you''re on a supplement yet. If you currently take an iron tablet, tea and coffee can meaningfully reduce how much of it you absorb when taken with or close to it, though the exact size of the effect varies with the beverage, the meal, and the timing. Follow whatever timing came with your own tablet; leaving around two hours from tea, coffee, dairy, and eggs is commonly advised. This doesn''t mean giving up tea, just spacing it out once your tablet begins. If you also take a calcium supplement once your iron tablet begins, keep that separation as before — this week''s tea-and-coffee gap is a separate, additional habit, not a replacement for it. Milk, curd, and other calcium-rich foods can also reduce iron absorption somewhat if eaten right alongside an iron-rich meal, but ordinary meals still don''t need to be organised around perfect separation — when you''re on a tablet, it and a sensible gap from tea or coffee do most of the real work. If this applies to you (hypothyroidism treated with levothyroxine): Iron supplements can reduce levothyroxine absorption. A four-hour separation is commonly advised, but follow the timing given by your doctor or pharmacist. Do not change either medicine on your own. Source: NHS guidance on ferrous sulfate; NHS Scotland Right Decisions ferrous sulfate medicine information.", "breakfast_a": "Poha or upma with a squeeze of lemon, and a citrus fruit on the side.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a boiled egg (cooked through) alongside.", "lunch_a": "Rajma or chana curry with rice, with a wedge of lemon squeezed over it.", "lunch_b": "The same rajma or chana curry with rice and lemon, with a portion of chicken curry (cooked through) added alongside.", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange, guava, or amla (Indian gooseberry) — all genuinely high in vitamin C.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana or peanuts.", "dinner_a": "A leafy-green sabzi with roti, cooked with tomato or a little lemon.", "dinner_b": "A fish curry (cooked through) with roti, plus a side of the same leafy-green sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A hunger-led snack — whatever you''d normally reach for."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Dal, gur, and greens — the everyday way", "notes": "Today: Jaggery, dals, and leafy greens are already iron-familiar staples in most Indian kitchens — small, ordinary choices rather than anything unusual. Jaggery contains real iron, though the amount varies a lot depending on how it''s processed — traditionally, some of it comes from the iron pots it''s boiled in, so its contribution isn''t as guaranteed as a measured food would be. It''s a pleasant way to add a little more, not something to rely on as a main source. This week doesn''t need to be a separate ''iron meal'' anyway — it''s the same dal, sabzi, and gur most households already have.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa or bajra roti with a little jaggery.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a boiled egg (cooked through) on the side.", "lunch_a": "Chana dal or rajma with rice and a methi or palak sabzi.", "lunch_b": "A mutton or chicken curry (cooked through) with rice, plus the same methi or palak sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Til-gur chikki (sesame and jaggery), or a handful of dates.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana or peanuts.", "dinner_a": "Lobia or moong dal curry with roti.", "dinner_b": "A fish curry (cooked through) with roti, plus a side of dal to keep today''s iron source in place.", "still_hungry": "A regular hunger-led snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Heme iron — where meat, fish, and egg genuinely help", "notes": "Today: Heme iron, found only in meat, poultry, and fish, is absorbed far more efficiently than the non-heme iron in plant foods — roughly 15-35% versus 2-20%. Eggs contain iron too, but it''s a non-heme form, so they add some value without matching meat or fish. This is a day where non-vegetarian choices genuinely add more than variety. Liver is one of the richest heme-iron sources there is, but for the same vitamin-A reason explained in Week 10, it''s better skipped in pregnancy — regular meat, poultry, and fish still provide genuine heme iron without that risk. If you eat mostly or only plant foods, non-heme iron plus a vitamin C source (Day 2) is still real — it''s just a somewhat less efficient pathway, which is part of why your prescribed tablet matters regardless of diet.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha or upma.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a boiled egg (cooked through) alongside — a real, if modest, iron contributor.", "lunch_a": "Rajma or chana curry with rice and a leafy-green sabzi.", "lunch_b": "A mutton, chicken, or fish curry (cooked through) with rice and a leafy-green sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Pumpkin and sesame seeds with guava or orange.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana with amla, mosambi, or another vitamin-C-rich fruit.", "dinner_a": "Palak chana, or a dal-and-greens combination, with roti.", "dinner_b": "A fish or mutton curry (cooked through) with roti and a side of dal or greens.", "still_hungry": "Fruit, or a familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "Today: Iron-friendly choices are still manageable when you''re not cooking yourself — and if you currently take an iron tablet, a sensible gap from chai is too. Make it easier: If you currently take an iron tablet away from home, carry it separately from any tea or coffee habit, and try to leave the gap even when the day gets busy — a missed gap once in a while isn''t something to worry over.", "breakfast_a": "Idli or dosa with sambar (lentil-based), plus a citrus fruit if available. Chai can still fit within your daily caffeine limit, but if you currently take an iron tablet, it''s worth leaving a gap from chai around when you take it.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a boiled egg alongside if it''s available.", "lunch_a": "A dal or rajma-based thali item, or a simple sabzi with rice.", "lunch_b": "A chicken or mutton dish (cooked through), with the same dal or sabzi alongside.", "nourishment_break_a": "A whole orange or guava carried with you.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana or peanuts carried with you.", "dinner_a": "A shared family meal — whatever''s on the table.", "dinner_b": "The same meal, with an extra spoon of dal or a squeeze of lemon added if you''d like an iron-absorption boost.", "still_hungry": "An extra roti, or a familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When your iron tablet causes constipation or nausea", "notes": "Today: If an iron tablet causes constipation or nausea, that''s a genuine, common side effect, especially at the doses used in pregnancy — not a sign you''re doing something wrong, and not a reason to stop it on your own. If you''re increasing fibre either way, do it gradually — a sudden jump can add bloating on top of what you''re already managing. A practical target is around 25g of fibre a day, increased gradually, and about 8-12 cups of water across the day — worth keeping up regardless of whether you''re on an iron tablet yet. If you are, taking it with a small non-dairy snack (like a plain cracker or a piece of fruit) rather than on an empty stomach can make it easier to tolerate, since dairy is one of the things worth keeping some distance from your tablet — food can help with nausea specifically, too. If constipation or nausea is persistent or uncomfortable, tell your care team rather than stopping the tablet yourself — there are safe options they can guide you through.", "breakfast_a": "Oats or dalia (broken wheat porridge) with chopped prunes or raisins.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a boiled egg (cooked through) alongside.", "lunch_a": "Rajma or chana curry (both fibre-rich) with a whole-grain roti and a cooked vegetable.", "lunch_b": "A chicken or fish curry (cooked through) with the same fibre-rich dal and vegetable.", "nourishment_break_a": "4-5 prunes, or a small bowl of papaya.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana or peanuts.", "dinner_a": "A leafy-green and lentil combination with roti.", "dinner_b": "A fish curry (cooked through) with roti, plus the same leafy-green and lentil side.", "still_hungry": "A familiar fibre-containing snack, with water alongside if you''d like."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "Today: A week of small iron habits is worth a quick look back — and a good moment to notice which iron-supporting habit felt easiest to fit into real life. Iron-rich foods support your everyday intake. India''s routine IFA programme generally begins in the second trimester, while some mothers may be prescribed iron earlier based on their blood tests or clinical needs. Take it exactly as directed and don''t start, stop, or change the dose without your care team.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week, vegetarian or not — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A piece of fruit, or another familiar snack."}]'::jsonb,
   'Which iron-friendly habit felt easiest to keep up this week — the tea-and-coffee gap, pairing a meal with vitamin C, or just noticing which foods already had iron in them?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at protein — how much you actually need, and why spreading it across the day tends to work better than one large serving.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- pregnancy week 12: Protein, spread through your day
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('pregnancy',
   12,
   'first',
   NULL,
   'Protein, spread through your day',
   '"A steady protein source at each meal supports you and your baby — no need to load it all into one big serving, or worry about combining plant proteins perfectly."',
   'Protein helps build your baby''s tissues and organs, and supports your own changing body throughout pregnancy. During the first trimester, the goal isn''t to force a large additional amount of protein — continue including varied protein foods across the day, much as you would outside pregnancy. Protein needs rise more clearly later on: ICMR-NIN''s 2020 guidelines list an additional 9.5g a day in the second trimester and 22g a day in the third. Dal, curd, paneer, tofu, soya chunks, eggs, meat, and fish are all real protein sources — the exact food matters less than eating some protein fairly regularly across your day, rather than skipping it at some meals and loading up at others. If you eat meat, fish, or eggs: Meat, fish, eggs, and dairy are naturally ''complete'' proteins, so if you eat them, protein planning is fairly simple by default. That doesn''t mean a vegetarian diet falls short — a varied plant-based diet across the day meets the same need, as Days 2 and 4 explain. Where liver would normally be a very rich protein source, the same vitamin-A caution from Weeks 10 and 11 still applies, so regular meat, poultry, fish, and eggs remain the better everyday choices.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Today: A protein source at each meal — dal, curd, paneer, egg, chicken, or fish — is a reasonable place to start. This early in pregnancy, that''s really about keeping a good pattern going, not chasing a bigger number — the real increase in protein needs comes later in pregnancy. Spreading it across the day, rather than saving it all for dinner, is still a reasonable habit to build now. If this applies to you (gestational diabetes): If you have diabetes or gestational diabetes, including a protein food alongside carbohydrate may help with fullness and may moderate the post-meal glucose response. Continue following the carbohydrate portions, meal timing, and glucose targets given by your care team. Source: general nutrition guidance for diabetes and gestational diabetes in pregnancy.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla or besan (chickpea flour) chilla.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a boiled egg (cooked through) alongside.", "lunch_a": "Roti, rajma or chana dal, and a cooked vegetable.", "lunch_b": "The same roti and vegetable, with a chicken or fish curry (cooked through) added alongside.", "nourishment_break_a": "Curd with fruit, or roasted chana.", "nourishment_break_b": "A boiled egg (cooked through), or a small piece of grilled chicken.", "dinner_a": "Paneer or tofu-and-vegetable curry with roti.", "dinner_b": "A fish or egg curry (cooked through) with roti.", "still_hungry": "Roasted chana, nuts, or another familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "No need to combine, no need to save it all for dinner", "notes": "Today: The idea that vegetarians must combine specific proteins at the same meal — like dal with rice — to get ''complete'' protein is outdated. Plant proteins don''t have to be deliberately paired in the same meal — eating enough food and including a variety of dals, beans, grains, nuts, seeds, and soy across the day generally provides the different amino acids the body needs. Spreading protein-containing foods across meals may also make it easier to meet your needs, stay full, and manage smaller meals if your appetite is unpredictable — it''s a flexible pattern that may help, not a rule to stress over or a requirement for a healthy pregnancy. None of this means dal-and-rice is a bad combination — it''s a good one. It just doesn''t need to be treated as the only correct way to eat protein. If this applies to you (hypothyroidism treated with levothyroxine): If you take levothyroxine, take it consistently exactly as prescribed. Soy foods can affect its absorption in some circumstances. If you regularly eat soy — or substantially change how much soy you eat — ask your doctor or pharmacist whether your medicine timing or thyroid monitoring needs adjustment. Source: NHS guidance on levothyroxine.", "breakfast_a": "Oats with milk and a handful of nuts.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a boiled egg (cooked through) on the side.", "lunch_a": "Rajma or chana curry with rice — no need to add a separate ''complementary'' protein.", "lunch_b": "A chicken or fish curry (cooked through) with rice.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd, or a glass of milk.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted peanuts or chana.", "dinner_a": "Dal and a vegetable with roti — a smaller, ordinary portion is enough.", "dinner_b": "An egg or chicken curry (cooked through) with roti, kept to a similar size as lunch rather than the day''s biggest serving.", "still_hungry": "A hunger-led snack — whatever you''d normally reach for."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Dal, curd, and everyday vegetarian protein", "notes": "Today: Dal, curd, paneer, chickpeas, and soya chunks are already familiar, everyday vegetarian protein staples in most Indian kitchens. If sprouts are on the menu, cook them through rather than eating them raw — raw sprouts carry a real food-safety risk that washing doesn''t remove. This doesn''t need to be a separate ''protein meal'' anyway — it''s the same dal, curd, and soya most households already cook.", "breakfast_a": "A well-cooked sprouted-moong chilla (not raw sprouts), or curd with fruit.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a boiled egg (cooked through) on the side.", "lunch_a": "Soya-chunk (soybean nugget) curry with rice or roti.", "lunch_b": "A chicken curry (cooked through) with rice or roti.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana, or a small bowl of curd.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted peanuts.", "dinner_a": "Rajma or chana dal with a cooked vegetable and roti.", "dinner_b": "A fish curry (cooked through) with roti and a cooked vegetable.", "still_hungry": "A regular hunger-led snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Complete protein, without needing to think about it", "notes": "Today: Animal proteins — egg, dairy, meat, and fish — each contain all the essential amino acids your body needs in one food, so they''re often called ''complete'' proteins. Most individual plant proteins are a little lower in one or two amino acids, but that''s not a shortfall to fear — eating enough food and a variety of plant proteins across your day (dal, legumes, grains, nuts, seeds) generally provides what your body needs, as Day 2 explained. This is a day where non-vegetarian choices are simple by default, not a day where the vegetarian path falls short.", "breakfast_a": "Idli or dosa with sambar and a side of curd.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a boiled egg (cooked through) alongside.", "lunch_a": "Rajma, chana, or soya-chunk curry with rice and a vegetable.", "lunch_b": "A chicken or fish curry (cooked through) with rice and a vegetable.", "nourishment_break_a": "A glass of milk, or curd with fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "A boiled egg (cooked through).", "dinner_a": "Paneer or tofu curry with roti and a dal side.", "dinner_b": "An egg or fish curry (cooked through) with roti.", "still_hungry": "Fruit, nuts, or a familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "Today: Protein choices are still findable when you''re not cooking yourself, whichever way you eat. Make it easier: Carry roasted chana, peanuts, or a boiled egg (in an insulated bag with an ice pack) for a protein-friendly snack that travels easily.", "breakfast_a": "Idli, dosa, or a dal-based breakfast, plus curd if available.", "breakfast_b": "The same, with a boiled egg alongside if it''s available.", "lunch_a": "A dal, rajma, or chana-based thali item with rice or roti.", "lunch_b": "A chicken or egg dish (cooked through), with the same dal or sabzi alongside.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana or peanuts carried with you.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small packet of curd or buttermilk, if refrigeration allows.", "dinner_a": "A shared family meal — whatever''s on the table.", "dinner_b": "The same meal, with an extra spoon of dal or a side of curd added for a protein boost.", "still_hungry": "An extra roti, or a familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When nausea makes big meals hard", "notes": "Today: If a full plate feels like too much right now, protein doesn''t need to come in one big serving — small, easy-to-tolerate portions spread through the day genuinely count, and this is exactly the kind of week where that habit helps most. On a difficult day, small amounts still count — choose whatever protein-containing food you can tolerate. If you cannot keep liquids down, cannot eat for 24 hours, are passing very little or dark urine, feel dizzy, or are losing weight, contact your care team promptly.", "breakfast_a": "A small bowl of curd or a glass of milk, with plain toast if that''s manageable.", "breakfast_b": "A few bites of a boiled egg (cooked through), if that feels tolerable.", "lunch_a": "A small bowl of thin, well-cooked moong dal with rice.", "lunch_b": "A few bites of plain, mildly seasoned chicken or fish (cooked through), if that''s easier to manage than dal.", "nourishment_break_a": "A few spoons of curd, or a small handful of nuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "A boiled egg (cooked through) — even just the white, if the yolk feels like too much.", "dinner_a": "A small bowl of dal or curd-based khichdi.", "dinner_b": "A little plain chicken or fish (cooked through) with rice, kept simple.", "still_hungry": "Whatever small, bland, protein-containing bite feels manageable — this isn''t a day to push for a full portion."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "Today: A week of small protein habits is worth a quick look back — and a good moment to notice whether spreading it across the day, rather than saving it for dinner, felt like an easier way to eat. Many mothers can meet their protein needs through varied everyday foods without counting every gram — especially this early, before the second- and third-trimester increases apply. Needs and intake still vary, especially when appetite is limited or certain food groups are avoided. The aim is regular inclusion, not very high protein intake or perfection.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week, vegetarian or not — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar protein-containing snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'Which protein habit felt easiest to keep up this week — spreading it across meals, letting go of the ''must combine'' pressure, or just noticing which foods already had protein in them?',
   'Next week, in our last First Trimester nourishment week, we''ll look at healthy fats — including omega-3/DHA sources, and why fat isn''t something to fear in pregnancy.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
